WEBVTT

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:27.100
<v Ira Glass>I got my teeth cleaned this week, and I was asking the hygienist how it was going with the global pandemic and all. And she was like, just yesterday somebody stole two face masks from the counter right there. And so I was like, do you know who? And she was like, yeah, she knew exactly who, because she caught her red handed, and the woman wasn't sorry at all-- wouldn't give the masks back. I mean, there's a national shortage, but still.
<v Ira Glass>这周我刚洗了牙，顺便问了下牙科保健员，在这场全球疫情之下工作感觉如何。她说，就在昨天，还有人从柜台上直接偷走了两个口罩。我问她知不知道是谁，她说她很清楚，因为当场就把人抓住了，那女的根本没表现出歉意，也不肯把口罩还回来。我的意思是，全国现在口罩短缺，但即便如此也不该这样。

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:50.810
<v Ira Glass>I heard about this woman, Kim, who decided a couple weeks ago to buy some face masks for her mom. Her mom works at a home caring for the elderly, and face masks are recommended for people like her mom. Kim went shopping for some. CVS-- sold out. Rite Aid-- sold out. Target-- sold out. She called pharmacies around town in northern California, where she lives. No luck, until she reached this one woman.
<v Ira Glass>我听说有个叫金的女士，几周前想给妈妈买些口罩。她妈妈在一家养老院工作，这类口罩是推荐给像她妈妈这样的人佩戴的。金去买的时候，CVS卖光了，Rite Aid卖光了，塔吉特也卖光了。她给北加州住处附近的药店都打过电话，但都没货，直到她联系上一位女士。

00:00:50.810 --> 00:01:03.230
<v Kim>She said they were sold out, and then she paused for a second. And then she said, well, I have a stash at home. I might be able to sell you some masks. Let me call you after work.
<v Kim>她说没货了，然后停顿了一下，接着说，不过我家里有些存货，或许可以卖给你一些，下班后再打给你。

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.690
<v Ira Glass>Wait, and so suddenly it's like you're doing an illegal drug deal or something over a mask?
<v Ira Glass>等等，这感觉怎么突然像是在做某种口罩的非法毒品交易一样？

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:22.870
<v Kim>Kind of felt that way. So I called her after work. We set up a time and place, and we met the following day in front of the Safeway, next to the Glacier water bottle vending machine.
<v Kim>确实有点那种感觉。我下班后给她打了电话，约好了时间和地点，第二天我们在西夫韦超市（Safeway）门口的冰川牌瓶装水自动贩卖机旁见了面。

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:23.480
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait.
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:23.980
<v Kim>And--
<v Kim>然后——

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:30.440
<v Ira Glass>Wait, that's what she said? She said, OK, we're going to meet in front of this Safeway in front of the Glacier bottle vending machine?
<v Ira Glass>等等，她真是这么说的？她说，好，我们就在西夫韦超市门口的冰川瓶装水自动贩卖机前见？

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:38.920
<v Kim>Yes. I found her easily, gave her the cash, got the box, said thank you--
<v Kim>对。我很轻松地找到了她，给了钱，拿到了盒子，说了谢谢——

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:51.830
<v Ira Glass>Now, describe the actual physical transaction. Now, when you handed her the cash, was it a kind of like your hand was over top of the cash so someone else couldn't see it, and she palmed it away from you.
<v Ira Glass>现在，描述一下实际的交易过程。当你把现金递给她的时候，是不是那种把手盖在钱上面以免被别人看见，然后她又从你手里把钱顺走的动作？

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:54.590
<v Kim>Pretty much, yeah.
<v Kim>差不多就是那样。

00:01:54.590 --> 00:02:03.890
<v Ira Glass>The woman asked for $100 for 10 masks. Kim talked her down to $70. Regular price would be $1 per mask, maybe $2.
<v Ira Glass>那个女人要价100美元买10个口罩，金最后砍价到70美元。正常价格应该是一个口罩1美元，顶多2美元。

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:20.190
<v Kim>And she told me that she had two boxes left. So if I wanted more, I could come back.
<v Kim>她告诉我她还剩两盒，如果我还想要的话，可以再来找她。

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:31.030
<v Cameron>I work at a local co-op here in Seattle, Washington called PCC Community Markets, just a little bit different clientele. They're kind of like a Whole Foods clientele, a little more particular.
<v Cameron>我在西雅图的一家当地合作社PCC社区市场工作，那里的顾客群体不太一样。他们有点像全食超市（Whole Foods）的顾客，稍微更挑剔一些。

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:45.450
<v Ira Glass>Cameron works in the produce department, and he's a cashier. Washington state, you may know, has more cases than any other state, and most of them are around Seattle. So the co-op started selling out of toilet paper, beans, flour, hand sanitizer when this first hit the news.
<v Ira Glass>卡梅伦在农产品部门工作，也是一名收银员。大家可能知道，华盛顿州的病例比其他任何州都要多，而且大多集中在西雅图。所以当新闻刚报道疫情时，合作社的卫生纸、豆子、面粉和洗手液就都卖光了。

00:02:45.450 --> 00:03:03.130
<v Cameron>It's been pretty tense, very just on edge. A lot of people are upset that we don't have hand sanitizer wipes for our carts. I've had a few customers vocally yell at me because of how I was touching their cans when I was checking them out at the register.
<v Cameron>气氛一直很紧张，大家都如履薄冰。很多人对我们购物车没有消毒湿巾感到不满。因为我在收银台结账时触碰了顾客的罐头，还有几位顾客对我大吼大叫。

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.520
<v Ira Glass>Like this one guy, who had a lot of cans.
<v Ira Glass>就像有个家伙，买了很多罐头。

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:27.690
<v Cameron>It was a kombucha energy drink. I can't remember the exact brand. He probably had a couple dozen. Some of them had fallen over, and I'd grabbed the top of the can versus the bottom of the can when scanning it, which, I mean, I can understand. But at the same time, I probably wasn't the first person to touch that can, so.
<v Cameron>那是一种康普茶能量饮料，我不记得具体牌子了。他可能买了二三十罐。有些倒了，我扫描时抓的是罐头顶部而不是底部，说实话，我能理解他的想法。但同时，我想我也不是第一个碰到那罐头的人吧。

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.360
<v Ira Glass>What did they say to you?
<v Ira Glass>他们对你说了什么？

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:49.550
<v Cameron>He just wanted me to be aware of what I was doing. It took me a minute to understand what he was saying, because he was so frustrated. I don't remember the exact words, but paraphrasing he was asking me in a pretty passive aggressive way why I'm touching the cans the way I am.
<v Cameron>他只是想让我注意自己的动作。我花了一会儿才明白他的意思，因为他当时非常沮丧。我不记得原话了，但转述一下，他用一种相当消极且具有攻击性的方式问我，为什么要那样拿罐头。

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:55.260
<v Ira Glass>Somehow that's exactly what I would have pictured. I'm picturing sort of Fred Armisen in Portlandia.
<v Ira Glass>不知为何，这场景我完全能想象得到。我脑子里浮现出《波特兰迪亚》里弗雷德·阿米森的样子。

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:16.470
<v Cameron>You're so spot on it's gross right now. [LAUGHS] He was wearing glasses, for sure, and some sort of fleece. But he was just telling me how I need to be touching the cans without touching the tops, and yeah.
<v Cameron>你形容得太到位了，简直让人反感。[笑声] 他肯定戴着眼镜，穿着某种抓绒衣。他就是在教育我以后拿罐头不能摸顶部，总之就是这样。

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:33.110
<v Ira Glass>Some people have more to fear than others right now, I think, like Jasmine Reese. She works at Los Angeles airport, LAX, cleaning out planes after they land, including international flights. Coronavirus became a very real thing to her over a month ago when a plane arrived from Hong Kong.
<v Ira Glass>我觉得现在有些人比其他人更恐慌，比如贾思敏·里斯。她在洛杉矶机场工作，负责在飞机降落后清理机舱，包括国际航班。一个多月前，一架从香港飞来的飞机降落时，新冠病毒对她来说变成了一件非常现实的事。

00:04:33.110 --> 00:05:00.500
<v Jasmine Reese>Even as I'm getting on the plane, and I see first class, business, I'm walking down. And I'm seeing co-workers with face masks, and I'm like, what's going on? Why does everybody have a face mask? They're like, oh, because there's a virus. There's a virus, and you have to clean this plane. This is one of the planes that, you know, this is Hong Kong. And I'm like, oh, let me go downstairs and go get me one, because keep in mind that I'm five months pregnant.
<v Jasmine Reese>即便在我上飞机时，我看到了头等舱和商务舱，我往下走，看到同事们都戴着口罩，我心想，怎么了？为什么每个人都戴口罩？他们说，哦，因为有病毒。有病毒，你得清理这架飞机。这可是香港飞来的飞机之一。我心想，哦，让我下楼去拿一个，因为别忘了我怀孕五个月了。

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.710
<v Ira Glass>And did they have face masks? Were you able to get one?
<v Ira Glass>那他们有口罩吗？你拿到了吗？

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:14.310
<v Jasmine Reese>Yeah, but we don't have them every day, and we work every day, but we're kind of low on supply, so meaning that we may not have face masks every day. We may not have gloves every day.
<v Jasmine Reese>有，但不是每天都有，我们每天都要工作，但供应有点短缺，意思就是我们可能不是每天都有口罩。我们可能不是每天都有手套。

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:19.100
<v Ira Glass>There have been days since the virus when you went in, and you had to clean a plane without a mask?
<v Ira Glass>自病毒爆发以来，有没有哪天你进去工作，却不得不没戴口罩清理飞机？

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:27.910
<v Jasmine Reese>Yeah, there's plenty. There's plenty of days that we get on there and we don't have no mask. We're cleaning. Sometimes we get on there. We don't have masks and gloves.
<v Jasmine Reese>有，很多次。很多天我们上去清理时都没有口罩。有时候我们上去清理，既没有口罩也没有手套。

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:45.770
<v Ira Glass>One job the crew does-- they reach deep into garbage bags on the plane and pull out the garbage by hand. Jasmine cleans American Airlines planes. She works for a contractor called Jetstream Ground Services. A number of Jetstream employees have been quoted in the press saying the same thing about gloves and masks that Jasmine says.
<v Ira Glass>机组人员有一项工作——他们得伸手进飞机上的垃圾袋里，用手把垃圾掏出来。贾思敏清理的是美国航空的飞机。她为一家叫Jetstream地勤服务的承包商工作。媒体多次援引多名Jetstream员工的话，说的都是和贾思敏一样关于手套和口罩的问题。

00:05:45.770 --> 00:06:02.540
<v Ira Glass>We reached out to a spokesperson for the company, Eric Rose. He said in emails, quote, "we are at a loss as to why the employees are making these false claims." Jetstream Ground Services has always had an abundant supply of safety equipment for all employees to fulfill their daily assignments." End quote.
<v Ira Glass>我们联系了该公司发言人埃里克·罗斯。他在邮件中写道，引用如下：“我们不解为何员工会提出这些不实指控。Jetstream地勤服务公司一直为所有员工提供充足的防护设备，以完成他们的日常工作。”引用结束。

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:19.130
<v Ira Glass>Just this week, the Service Employees International Union started distributing masks and gloves to workers like Jasmine in LAX, saying Jetstream has not properly provided them. The day I talked to Jasmine, two people who work at LAX had at that point tested positive with the virus. Jasmine thought one of them worked at terminal seven.
<v Ira Glass>就在本周，服务业雇员国际工会开始向贾思敏等洛杉矶机场的员工分发口罩和手套，称Jetstream没有为他们提供妥善的保护。在我与贾思敏交谈的那天，洛杉矶机场有两名员工当时已经确诊感染了病毒。贾思敏认为其中一人在七号航站楼工作。

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:27.570
<v Jasmine Reese>So we have a person all the way down at seven that got sick, and then now they're saying a person, I think, from terminal five was sick. We're at four.
<v Jasmine Reese>所以七号航站楼已经有一个人病倒了，现在他们又说五号航站楼也有人病了。我们在四号。

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:27.890
<v Ira Glass>Damn.
<v Ira Glass>天哪。

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:35.700
<v Jasmine Reese>So we're right next to a person that could have possibly came to the food court at our terminal, you just never know.
<v Jasmine Reese>所以我们离一个可能去过我们航站楼美食广场的人很近，你永远不知道会发生什么。

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.960
<v Ira Glass>Do you feel like it's only a matter of time before somebody you work with gets sick?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得你身边的人病倒只是时间问题吗？

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:57.480
<v Jasmine Reese>Yeah, it's how it's going, and it's on the news, and it's spreading so wide. I wouldn't be surprised if someone-- if the news popped up and said somebody is sick within the next week or two. I could be walking around with a co-worker right now, and they probably have it. You don't know that they have it.
<v Jasmine Reese>是的，情况就是这样，新闻里都在报，而且传播范围这么广。如果新闻突然报道说未来一两周内有同事病倒了，我一点也不会惊讶。我现在走来走去，身边的同事可能就已经感染了，你根本不知道谁带毒。

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.960
<v Ira Glass>So when you're cleaning planes, is this what you're thinking about all the time?
<v Ira Glass>所以当你清理飞机时，你一直在想这个问题吗？

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:09.270
<v Jasmine Reese>If it's internationally, yes. I'm thinking, why do you have clean this plane? And I'm trying not to stress on it too hard.
<v Jasmine Reese>如果是国际航班，是的。我在想，为什么我非得清理这架飞机？我尽量不去想得太沉重。

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:15.630
<v Ira Glass>And I can't totally tell. How worried are you?
<v Ira Glass>我完全看不出来，你有多担心？

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:26.830
<v Jasmine Reese>Truth-- I'm very worried, but I've still got to work and get my money. I have to survive, yes, but I am very worried about it, certainly because I am pregnant.
<v Jasmine Reese>说实话，我非常担心，但我还得工作赚钱。我得生存，是的，但我确实非常担心，尤其是因为我怀孕了。

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:53.550
<v Ira Glass>Right now as I'm saying these words to you, dozens of people have already died in our country, but somehow it still feels like the calm before the storm. For now, it's like streets are a little emptier, store shelves are emptier. Some people are working from home. Some schools close. Other schools-- everybody's waiting to hear if they're going to be called off. Things are not as awful as they're certainly going to get, so we're all waiting for it to get awful, with no idea of how awful that's going to be.
<v Ira Glass>就在我跟你说这些话的时候，国内已经有几十人死亡了，但不知为何，感觉仍然像是暴风雨前的宁静。现在，街道稍微空了一些，商店货架也空了一些。有些人居家办公，有些学校关门了。其他学校——大家都在等通知是否会停课。情况还远没有到一定会变得那么糟糕的地步，所以我们都在等着那最糟糕的时刻到来，却完全不知道那到底会有多糟糕。

00:07:53.550 --> 00:08:17.290
<v Ira Glass>If I had to say what this feels like, I think to me what this feels like is-- I don't know-- the only experience I've ever had like this is sort of like waiting for medical results, trying to find out how bad things are going to be, but on a global scale. We're going to be documenting more about coronavirus in upcoming shows, but for today's show, we thought we would stick to this one part of things, this low hum of menace.
<v Ira Glass>如果非要说这种感觉是什么，我想我唯一有过类似经历的情况，就是等待医疗检查结果的时候，想知道病情到底会有多严重，只不过这次是全球范围的。我们在接下来的节目中会记录更多关于新冠病毒的内容，但今天的节目，我们想先关注这件事的这一面——那种低沉的威胁感。

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:41.440
<v Ira Glass>Here in our staff we were talking about when else does somebody actually feel that feeling? And we found some examples of that to share with you, and that's going to be our show today. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们的员工也讨论过，人们到底是在什么时候真的会有这种感觉？我们找到了一些案例来分享，这就是我们今天的节目。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:41.440 --> 00:09:00.630
<v Ira Glass>Act One-- Do You Hear What I Hear? So this first story is about a recording that captures this exact thing today's program is about, that low hum of menace. The recording was made secretly by a woman trying to catch a man who'd been harassing her. Heads up to listeners, this story is about sexual harassment, so there's some sexual content to this.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——我听到的你听到了吗？第一个故事是关于一段录音，它捕捉到了今天节目所讨论的——那种低沉的威胁感。这段录音是一位试图抓到骚扰她的男子的女性秘密录制的。在此提醒听众，这个故事涉及性骚扰，所以会有些性内容。

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:33.520
<v Ira Glass>One thing I have to say that's interesting about this recording and the man's behavior in it is how understated it is. He doesn't raise his voice. He doesn't overtly threaten, but he just gets his point across. Also interesting, the woman who made the recording-- her name is Khristen Sellers. Years later, today, when she hears this recording, she hears it very differently than she did at the time. It raises a whole set of questions she didn't think about much back then. Reporter Jessica Lussenhop met Khristen years ago putting together a story for the BBC. Here's Jessica.
<v Ira Glass>关于这段录音以及那名男子的行为，有一点很有趣，那就是他的表达非常含蓄。他没有提高嗓门，也没有公开威胁，但他却准确地表达了他的意思。还有一点很有趣，录制这段录音的女性，她的名字叫克莉丝汀·塞勒斯。多年后的今天，当她再听这段录音时，感受与当时截然不同。它引发了一系列她当年没怎么考虑过的问题。记者杰西卡·卢森霍普几年前在为英国广播公司撰写报道时遇到了克莉丝汀。下面是杰西卡。

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:45.010
<v Jessica Lussenhop>The recording is just 10 minutes long. The man Khristen was trying to record was her home inspector. This is back in 2012. At the time, she didn't have a cell phone, so she borrowed one from her friend.
<v Jessica Lussenhop>这段录音只有10分钟。克莉丝汀试图录音的对象是她的房屋检查员。那是2012年的事了。当时她没有手机，所以借了朋友的手机。

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:49.550
<v Jessica Lussenhop>Can you just sort of explain how you made this recording in the first place?
<v Jessica Lussenhop>你能简单解释一下你最初是怎么录下这段录音的吗？

00:09:49.550 --> 00:10:07.300
<v Khristen Sellers>OK. I had on some around the house cleanup clothing, like baggy clothing. So I had a phone, and I took the phone, and I put it in my shirt. Put it in, I think, it was my bra strap or something, sitting some type of way odd.
<v Khristen Sellers>好的。我当时穿着打扫屋子用的衣服，比如宽松的衣服。我拿了手机，把它塞进衬衫里。把它塞进……我想是文胸肩带还是什么地方，总之是塞在一个很奇怪的位置。

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:23.320
<v Jessica Lussenhop>Because of the phone under the shirt secured by a bra strap, the recording is hard to hear. Hiding the phone there, Khristen's nervous. She gives herself a task-- raking the yard-- as she waits for Eric Pender to pull up. He is who she wants to record.
<v Jessica Lussenhop>由于手机压在衬衫里并用文胸肩带固定，录音听起来很困难。把手机藏在那里，克莉丝汀很紧张。她给了自己一个任务——耙院子里的草——等待埃里克·彭德到来。他就是她想录音的目标。

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:43.330
<v Jessica Lussenhop>She's kind of afraid of him, but she's focused on all the things anyone making this kind of recording might be. She wants to stay calm, not look suspicious, act natural. And most importantly, she wants to get audio of Eric Pender harassing her so she has undeniable proof. Eric Pender's red truck pulls up and parks on the street.
<v Jessica Lussenhop>她其实有点怕他，但她专注于任何做这种录音的人都会关注的事情。她想保持冷静，看起来不那么可疑，举止自然。最重要的是，她想录下埃里克·彭德骚扰她的音频，作为不可辩驳的证据。埃里克·彭德的红色卡车开过来，停在了街上。

00:10:43.330 --> 00:11:02.670
<v Khristen Sellers>There was a long driveway. So I'm in a wooded area, ain't no whole bunch of houses near me or nothing, and he was walking up. But I was still raking, doing both. And he was coming up towards me, and I was kind of trying to turn the other way. That's when he kind of got real close, and he was like, I'm--
